    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Traced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorm-the-old">Gorm the Old</a> around 936, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> is among the oldest continuous monarchies in the world.</li><li>Its kings ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut</a>, led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a> of 1397 and taxed Baltic shipping through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">the Sound</a> Toll.</li><li>Defeats by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1658 and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1864 left a small state that rebuilt itself as a welfare democracy.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Royal Law of 1665, which made the Danish king absolute, was not published until 1709 and is often called the only written constitution of European absolutism.</p></aside>
    </section>

