    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gudfred"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gudfred.jpg" alt="Gudfred" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gudfred">Gudfred</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kong_G%C3%B8trik_den_Gavmilde.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Erico Olai Tormio (publisher), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Danish kings appear in Frankish sources from the late eighth century: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudfred">Godfred</a> defied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> and strengthened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danevirke">Danevirke</a>, the earthwork across the base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> peninsula, around 808. But the continuous line of the Danish monarchy is traced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorm-the-old">Gorm the Old</a>, who ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jelling">Jelling</a> in central Jutland from about 936 until his death around 958. Gorm raised a runestone at Jelling in memory of his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thyra">Thyra</a>, one of the earliest monuments to name the land as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>.</p><p>His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-bluetooth">Harald Bluetooth</a> (r. c. 958–c. 986) set up the larger Jelling stone, which declares that he “won for himself all Denmark and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> Christian”. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald</a>’s conversion, usually dated to about 965 and linked by the chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind-of-corvey">Widukind of Corvey</a> to the missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poppo-of-frisia">Poppo</a>, tied the kingdom to the German church. Harald also built the bridge at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravning-bridge">Ravning Enge</a> and the circular fortresses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trelleborg-slagelse">Trelleborg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fyrkat">Fyrkat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aggersborg">Aggersborg</a>, which tree-ring dating places around 980. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-of-bremen">Adam of Bremen</a> his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweyn-forkbeard">Sweyn Forkbeard</a> rebelled and drove him into exile, where he died. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweyn-haakonsson">Sweyn</a>’s raids on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> culminated in its conquest in 1013, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut the Great</a> ruled England from 1016 and Denmark from about 1018, adding Norway in 1028.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ribe"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ribe.jpg" alt="Ribe" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ribe">Ribe</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RibeVue.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hjart, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> was a land of grain-growing villages on the fertile clays of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a>, the islands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a>, with fisheries that boomed when the Scania herring market drew merchants from across northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> each autumn from the twelfth century. Towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribe">Ribe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roskilde">Roskilde</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lund">Lund</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> were small by continental standards, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> dominated much of their trade. Lund became the seat of an archbishopric for all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> in 1103–1104, and the church grew into the largest landowner. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-of-pomerania">Eric of Pomerania</a> introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">the Sound</a> Toll around 1429, a levy on ships passing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingor">Helsingør</a> that helped fund the crown until its abolition in 1857.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Lutheran Reformation</a> of 1536 the crown seized church lands, and the nobility consolidated large manors worked by dependent peasants; the <em>stavnsbånd</em> of 1733 bound male peasants to their home estates until the agrarian reforms of 1788. Trading companies operated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tharangambadi">Tranquebar</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osu-castle">Christiansborg</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-virgin-islands">Danish West Indies</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-croix">St Croix</a> depended on enslaved Africans. Denmark declared the transatlantic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> illegal in 1792, with effect from 1803. Cooperative dairies and the folk high schools inspired by N.F.S. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/n-f-s-grundtvig">Grundtvig</a> later transformed rural life, and the twentieth century built a tax-funded welfare state.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lindanise"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-lindanise.jpg" alt="Battle of Lindanise" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lindanise">Battle of Lindanise</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Danmarks_flag_1219_Lorentzen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Christian August Lorentzen, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Viking-age Danish power rested on fleets and royal fortresses, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweyn-forkbeard">Sweyn Forkbeard</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut</a> raised the armies that extracted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danegeld">Danegeld</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Under Valdemar I the Great and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absalon">Absalon</a>, Danish fleets fought the Wendish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> of the southern Baltic and in 1168 took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkona">Arkona</a> on Rügen, destroying the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svantevit">Svantevit</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-ii-of-denmark">Valdemar II</a> “the Victorious” led a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, where, according to a legend first recorded in the sixteenth century, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flag-of-denmark">Dannebrog</a> flag fell from heaven during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lindanise">Battle of Lyndanisse</a> in 1219. His capture by Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-i-count-of-schwerin">Henry of Schwerin</a> in 1223 and defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bornhoved">Battle of Bornhöved</a> in 1227 ended Danish dominance over northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-iv-of-denmark">Valdemar IV Atterdag</a> sacked Visby on Gotland in 1361 but lost the ensuing war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>, ended by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-stralsund-1370">Treaty of Stralsund</a> in 1370.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> later fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> repeatedly for the Baltic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-iv-of-denmark">Christian IV</a> won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-war">Kalmar War</a> of 1611–1613 but was beaten by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-tserclaes-count-of-tilly">Tilly</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lutter">Battle of Lutter am Barenberge</a> in 1626 during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years’ War, and the Torstenson War ended in the losses of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bromsebro-1645">Treaty of Brömsebro</a> in 1645. In 1658 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-x-gustav">Charles X Gustav</a> of Sweden marched his army across the frozen belts. The navy remained formidable until the British attacked it at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-copenhagen-1801">Battle of Copenhagen</a> in 1801 and seized it in 1807. Defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dybbol">Dybbøl</a> in 1864 ended its military ambitions.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jutland-code"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jutland-code.jpg" alt="Jutland Code" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jutland-code">Jutland Code</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Landskabslove.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vesconte, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The medieval king was chosen by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> and acclaimed at regional assemblies (<em>landsting</em>), usually from the royal family. Provincial laws were written down in the thirteenth century, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland-code">Jutland Code</a> of 1241 issued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-ii-of-denmark">Valdemar II</a>, which opens with the words “with law shall the land be built”. From 1282 kings had to accept a coronation charter (<em>håndfæstning</em>) limiting their power, and the council of the realm (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigsradet">Rigsrådet</a></em>) of nobles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> took a large share in government. The crisis of the 1320s, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-ii-of-denmark">Christopher II</a> pawned most of the kingdom to the counts of Holstein, left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> without a king from 1332 to 1340. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-i-of-denmark">Margaret I</a> restored royal authority and, through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a> of 1397, ruled all three Scandinavian kingdoms in the name of her grandnephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-of-pomerania">Eric of Pomerania</a>.</p><p>The Oldenburg dynasty, beginning with Christian I in 1448, governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> as part of a composite monarchy and acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> and Holstein in 1460. In 1660 Frederick III used the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> and clergy to make the crown hereditary and absolute, codified in the Royal Law (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kings-law">Kongeloven</a></em>) of 1665, and Christian V’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danish-code">Danish Law</a> of 1683 unified the legal system. The Constitution of 5 June 1849 created a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>, and since 1901 governments have answered to the elected chamber, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/folketing">Folketing</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rosenborg-castle"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rosenborg-castle.jpg" alt="Rosenborg Castle" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rosenborg-castle">Rosenborg Castle</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:I12_174_Schloss_Rosenborg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Falk2, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Danish kings shaped the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut</a>’s reign bound <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> for a generation, and medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and crusading warfare into the Baltic; Danish rule of Reval (Tallinn) from 1219 to 1346 left its mark on the Estonian capital. The union of crowns with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> from 1380 to 1814 made Danish the written language of Norway, and Iceland, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroe Islands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a> stayed under the Danish crown after Norway’s departure, so that Danish administration and language shaped the whole North Atlantic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">The Sound</a> Toll made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> a crossroads of European diplomacy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-iv-of-denmark">Christian IV</a>’s buildings, among them the Round Tower and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosenborg-castle">Rosenborg Castle</a>, still define the city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tycho-brahe">Tycho Brahe</a>’s observatory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uraniborg">Uraniborg</a> on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ven-island">Hven</a>, granted to him by Frederick II in 1576, produced the observations from which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-kepler">Johannes Kepler</a> derived his laws of planetary motion.</p><p>Later Danish figures of wide influence include the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soren-kierkegaard">Søren Kierkegaard</a>, the writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hans-christian-andersen">Hans Christian Andersen</a> and the physicist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niels-bohr">Niels Bohr</a>, whose Copenhagen institute became a centre of quantum physics. In the twentieth century the rescue of most Danish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in October 1943, the Nordic welfare model and an early commitment to wind power gave a small state an influence well beyond its size.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/scanian-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/scanian-war.jpg" alt="Scanian War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/scanian-war">Scanian War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scanian_War_Collage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Imonoz, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> shrank from a Baltic great power to a small nation-state through a series of losses. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-roskilde">Treaty of Roskilde</a> in 1658 surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halland">Halland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blekinge">Blekinge</a>, the rich eastern provinces, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scanian-war">Scanian War</a> of 1675–1679 failed to win them back. Having sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> after 1807, Denmark went bankrupt in 1813 and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kiel">Treaty of Kiel</a> of 1814 ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> to Sweden, keeping Iceland, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroe Islands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>. The national conflict over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> and Holstein led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-schleswig-war">First Schleswig War</a> of 1848–1851 and the Second Schleswig War of 1864, after which the Treaty of Vienna gave both duchies and Lauenburg to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. The saying “what is lost outwardly must be won inwardly” captured the small kingdom’s turn to land reclamation and education.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-virgin-islands">Danish West Indies</a> were sold to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1917, and northern Schleswig returned after a plebiscite in 1920. Iceland became a separate kingdom in union with Denmark in 1918 and a republic in 1944. After the German occupation of 9 April 1940 to May 1945 Denmark became a founding member of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in 1949 and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-communities">European Community</a> in 1973. The Faroe Islands gained home rule in 1948 and Greenland in 1979. Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margrethe-ii">Margrethe II</a> abdicated in January 2024 in favour of her son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederik-x">Frederik X</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> is one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>’s oldest continuous kingdoms: from Viking-age <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jelling">Jelling</a> its kings ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and the approaches to the Baltic, taxed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">the Sound</a> for four centuries, and after losing most of their realm remade it as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> and welfare state that still includes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroe Islands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>.</em></p>

